1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,430
If you tried to unroll this,
it could crumble in your hands.

2
00:00:03,470 --> 00:00:06,050
So researchers unrolled
it inside a computer.

3
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:08,140
That black lump is a book.

4
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:11,610
It was buried when Mount Vesuvius
erupted in the year seventy-nine.

5
00:00:11,770 --> 00:00:14,130
Centuries later, people tried to open it.

6
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:18,040
By the time they were done, the roll was
less than half as wide as it had been.

7
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,880
And the writing they wanted
was still wrapped up inside.

8
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,600
Look at what happens when
you scan what’s left.

9
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:25,900
The researchers took X-ray
images from different

10
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:28,790
angles and used them to build
a three-dimensional map.

11
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,790
Slice through that map
and you can see a spiral.

12
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:35,890
That’s the sheet of papyrus,
wound around itself over and over.

13
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:39,780
Pick one of those thin lines and try
to follow it all the way around.

14
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,040
See where two layers press together?

15
00:00:42,610 --> 00:00:44,490
If you jump to the
neighboring layer there,

16
00:00:44,530 --> 00:00:47,030
you’ve just skipped
an entire turn of the page.

17
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:49,710
Keep going and you could end
up putting two pieces

18
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:52,770
of writing side by side that
were never next to each other.

19
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:55,180
So they traced the sheet through the scan,

20
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:58,230
checking and correcting the places
where the layers got messy.

21
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:01,040
That gave them a digital
surface they could flatten.

22
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:02,700
Now they had a page.

23
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:04,610
It looked almost blank.

24
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,460
The ink was carbon-based,
and the heat had turned

25
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,130
the papyrus into a carbon-rich
material too.

26
00:01:10,570 --> 00:01:13,610
In the scan, the two
could look almost identical.

27
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:18,130
So the team trained a model to pick
out signs of ink in tiny patches.

28
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:20,830
Each patch was smaller
than a single letter.

29
00:01:21,370 --> 00:01:25,250
Imagine trying to find the paint on
a road by looking at one pebble at a time.

30
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:27,520
One patch could look like a smudge.

31
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:31,730
Put the patches together and you can
start to see which marks form a stroke.

32
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:33,640
And here’s how they checked it.

33
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:38,370
On a second scroll, they already had text
that an earlier model had helped reveal.

34
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:42,330
A sharper scan of that scroll
picked up the ink deposits directly.

35
00:01:42,730 --> 00:01:44,230
They lined the two images up.

36
00:01:44,670 --> 00:01:47,310
The same strokes were
sitting in the same places.

37
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:49,870
Back on the first scroll,
the flattened sheet

38
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:52,050
stretched to about four and a half feet.

39
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:55,810
Scholars could read across roughly
twenty-two partial columns.

40
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:58,340
There are gaps where
the papyrus is missing.

41
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:02,630
One of the surviving passages is
about what people can gain through study.

42
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:04,880
Here’s a fragment of the translation.

43
00:02:05,490 --> 00:02:08,490
“Possessing the same
practical wisdom as they do.”

44
00:02:09,540 --> 00:02:13,300
Somebody put those words down by hand
more than two thousand years ago.

45
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:16,090
You just read a line
from inside the scroll.

46
00:02:16,690 --> 00:02:18,500
And the page is still rolled up.
